sentences.t:
μῆτερ ἐμή, τί νύ μ’ οὐ μίμνεις ἑλέειν μεμαῶτα,
ὄφρα καὶ εἰν Ἀίδαο φίλας περὶ χεῖρε βαλόντε
ἀμφοτέρω κρυεροῖο τεταρπώμεσθα γόοιο;

sentences.tr:
mēter emē, ti ny m’ ou mimneis heleein memaōta,
ophra kai ein Aidao philas peri cheire balonte
amphoterō kryeroio tetarpōmestha gooio;

sentences.l:
‘Oh me, my child, beyond all men ill-fated one,
not at all does Persephone, the daughter of Zeus, deceive you,
but this is the way of mortals, whenever someone may die;

There truly first Tyro I saw, the well-fathered,
who said she was of blameless Salmoneus the offspring,
and said she was the wife of Cretheus, Aeolus’ son;

and as the year goes round
you will bear splendid children, since not fruitless are the beds
of immortals;

and she, having conceived, bore Pelias and Neleus,
who as strong attendants of great Zeus became,
both of them;

And after her Alcmene I saw, the wife of Amphitryon,
who Heracles, bold-purposed, lion-hearted,
bore, in the arms of great Zeus having lain;

And the mother of Oedipus I saw, fine Epicaste,
who a great deed did in the ignorance of her mind,
having married her own son;

but she went to the (house) of Hades, gate-fastener, the strong,
having fastened a steep noose from the high roof-beam,
gripped by her own grief;

and Neleus would not give her
to him who should not drive crumple-horned, broad-browed cattle
from Phylace, from the force of Iphicles,
grievous;

who even beneath the earth honour from Zeus having,
at one time live on alternate days, at another time again
are dead;

Otus, godlike, and far-famed Ephialtes,
whom indeed the tallest reared the grain-giving plough-land,
and by far the fairest, after famous Orion;
